"hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:"
946,"We are told by Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala, Shuaib, meaning Ibn al-Layth, informed us, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, on the authority of Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr. Ibn Abd al-Rahman means, on the authority of Umayyah ibn Abd Allah ibn Khalid, that he said to Abd Allah ibn Umar: “We find the urban prayer and the Fear is mentioned in the Qur’an, and we do not find the journey prayer in the Qur’an? Abdullah said: O my nephew, God Almighty has sent to us Muhammad, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him. We do not know anything, we are only doing what we saw Muhammad, may God bless him and grant him peace, doing.”"
947,"Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Hakam al-Warraq informed us, Yahya ibn Sulaym informed us, on the authority of Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, on the authority of Nafi`, on the authority of Ibn Umar, He said: “I traveled with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, and with Abu Bakr, Omar, and Uthman, and they used to pray the noon and afternoon prayers, two rak’ahs.” Two rak'ahs, they do not pray before it or after it."" Abdullah bin Omar said: If I had been praying before or after it, I would have completed it."
948,"Abu Bakr said: And in the news of Anas bin Malik, “The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed the noon prayer in Medina for four days, and the afternoon prayer in Dhul-Hulayfah.” “Two rak’ahs” indicates that the believer who is not afraid of being tempted by the infidels has the right to shorten the prayer."
949,"Likewise the report of Haritha ibn Wahb, “The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed with us two rak’ahs as many times as we have ever done, and he was safe,” and the report of Abu Hanzalah, On the authority of Ibn Umar, I said: We are safe. He said: Likewise, the Sunnah of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, indicates that it is not permissible for someone who is not afraid to shorten prayer while traveling."
